('NGA', '566', 'Nigeria', 8, 9.5, 50, 65, 45, 55, 30, array['Crude','Prod','LNG','Gas']::text[], 'Africa''s largest crude exporter by capacity and, since the Dangote refinery reached full rate in 2026, a net exporter of refined products. Pipeline sabotage and theft in the Niger Delta remain the main physical risk; emergency rule in Rivers State in 2025 showed how quickly politics reaches the pipelines.', array['Dangote crude sourcing and product export volumes','Trans Niger and Trans Forcados pipeline security','NNPC refinery restarts and subsidy politics','2027 general election positioning']::text[], '[["2027-02", "General election due"]]'::jsonb, array['energy']::text[], 'africa'),
('LBY', '434', 'Libya', 17.5, 27, 85, 80, 70, 70, 40, array['Crude','Gas','Prod']::text[], 'Two rival authorities and armed groups that can close a field or a valve at will. Output swings between 1.2 million b/d and half that within weeks; 2026 brought a Sharara pipeline shutdown and force majeure at Zawiya, then valve closures at Hamada and Tahara.', array['Sharara–Zawiya pipeline and force majeure status','Central bank and revenue disputes between Tripoli and Benghazi','Guard and militia closures at NC fields','Greenstream gas flows to Italy']::text[], '[["2026-12", "NOC output target review"], ["2027", "Elections repeatedly postponed; no firm date"]]'::jsonb, array['energy']::text[], 'africa'),
('DZA', '012', 'Algeria', 2.5, 28, 40, 30, 55, 30, 20, array['Gas','LNG','Crude','LPG']::text[], 'Europe''s third-largest gas supplier through Transmed, Medgaz and the Arzew and Skikda LNG plants. Politically stable under tight state control; contract terms are set by Sonatrach and shift with Algiers'' diplomatic moods.', array['Pipeline gas volumes to Italy and Spain','Relations with Morocco and Spain affecting Medgaz','Upstream licensing round results']::text[], '[["2027", "Hydrocarbons law revisions expected"]]'::jsonb, array['energy']::text[], 'africa'),
('EGY', '818', 'Egypt', 30, 27, 45, 35, 45, 30, 20, array['Gas','LNG','Crude']::text[], 'Owner of the Suez Canal and SUMED pipeline, and now a gas importer after Zohr declined. The 2025 Israel–Iran war cut Israeli gas supply and forced LNG imports through floating terminals; Suez revenue tracks Red Sea security.', array['Suez Canal transit recovery','Israeli gas deliveries and LNG import tenders','Foreign-exchange availability for fuel imports']::text[], '[["2027", "Zohr and West Delta decline-management milestones"]]'::jsonb, array['energy']::text[], 'africa'),
('MRT', '478', 'Mauritania', -10.5, 20, 40, 35, 45, 40, 15, array['LNG','Fe']::text[], 'New LNG exporter since the Greater Tortue Ahmeyim project''s first cargo in 2025, and a long-standing iron ore exporter through SNIM. Stable by Sahel standards; security risk is spillover from Mali.', array['GTA phase 2 decisions','SNIM iron ore volumes via Nouadhibou','Sahel spillover on the eastern border']::text[], '[["2029", "Presidential election due"]]'::jsonb, array['metals','energy']::text[], 'africa'),
('GNQ', '226', 'Equatorial Guinea', 10.3, 1.6, 55, 25, 55, 35, 30, array['LNG','Crude']::text[], 'Declining oil producer trying to keep the Punta Europa LNG plant fed with gas from neighbouring fields. Long-serving ruler; succession and governance risk dominate.', array['Gas supply to Punta Europa (Alen, Aseng, cross-border)','Succession politics']::text[], '[["2027", "Legislative elections due"]]'::jsonb, array['energy']::text[], 'africa'),
('COG', '178', 'Congo-Brazzaville', 15.5, -0.8, 50, 35, 50, 45, 25, array['Crude','LNG']::text[], 'Oil producer with new floating LNG exports from the Marine XII block. Heavily indebted; contract and payment risk more than security.', array['Congo LNG second phase volumes','Debt restructuring and fuel payment arrears']::text[], '[["2026", "Presidential election cycle"]]'::jsonb, array['energy']::text[], 'africa'),
('TCD', '148', 'Chad', 18.5, 15.5, 65, 70, 60, 65, 35, array['Crude']::text[], 'Landlocked producer exporting through the Chad–Cameroon pipeline to Kribi. Nationalised ExxonMobil''s former assets in 2023; Sudan''s war spills over the eastern border.', array['Pipeline tariff disputes with Cameroon','Sudan spillover and refugee flows','Arbitration over the 2023 nationalisation']::text[], '[]'::jsonb, array['energy']::text[], 'africa'),
('SSD', '728', 'South Sudan', 30, 7.5, 85, 85, 75, 85, 55, array['Crude']::text[], 'All crude leaves through Sudan''s pipeline to Port Sudan, so Sudan''s war controls South Sudan''s income. Exports stopped in 2024, resumed at reduced rates in 2025, and remain hostage to the fighting.', array['Bashayer pipeline and Port Sudan security','Peace deal implementation and elections','Arrears to Chinese and Malaysian operators']::text[], '[["2026-12", "Elections scheduled (repeatedly delayed)"]]'::jsonb, array['energy']::text[], 'africa'),
('MAR', '504', 'Morocco', -6, 32, 20, 15, 25, 25, 10, array['Bunk','Prod']::text[], 'No domestic refining since 2015; Tangier Med is the main bunkering and transshipment hub at the Gibraltar gateway. Low political risk; relevance is logistics.', array['Tangier Med capacity and bunker supply','Nigeria–Morocco gas pipeline progress']::text[], '[["2026-09", "Legislative elections"]]'::jsonb, array['energy']::text[], 'africa'),
('BRA', '076', 'Brazil', -53, -10.5, 40, 40, 40, 45, 15, array['Fe','Bx','Au','Cu','Ni','Li','Mn','Crude','Prod']::text[], 'The world''s second-largest iron ore exporter and a 3.5 million b/d pre-salt oil producer. Policy risk is incremental rather than abrupt: tax changes, licensing delays and tailings-dam rules. The October 2026 general election sets the tone for Petrobras pricing, the Equatorial Margin and mining royalties.', array['Post-election direction on Petrobras fuel pricing and the Equatorial Margin (desk to confirm result)','Vale''s Carajás expansion and dam-safety decommissioning schedule','US tariff regime on Brazilian goods and the exemption list','Rail and port capacity at Ponta da Madeira and Santos']::text[], '[["2026-10-04", "General election, first round (runoff 25 October); desk to confirm outcome"], ["2027-01-01", "New federal administration takes office"], ["2027", "Petrobras drilling results on FZA-M-59, Equatorial Margin"]]'::jsonb, array['metals','energy']::text[], 'americas'),
('ARG', '032', 'Argentina', -65, -35, 45, 20, 50, 45, 15, array['Li','Cu','Au','Ag','Crude','Gas','LNG']::text[], 'Vaca Muerta has turned Argentina into a crude exporter and, from 2027, a prospective LNG exporter; the Puna hosts the second-largest lithium pipeline in the triangle. The RIGI incentive regime (2024) anchors large projects, but currency controls, provincial politics and the 2027 presidential cycle keep regulatory risk elevated.', array['RIGI approvals for copper (Vicuña, Los Azules) and lithium projects','Peso regime and export-duty changes','VMOS pipeline and Punta Colorada terminal timeline','Southern Energy FLNG first cargo (target 2027)']::text[], '[["2027-01", "Oldelval / VMOS expansion capacity milestones"], ["2027-10", "Presidential and legislative elections"]]'::jsonb, array['metals','energy']::text[], 'americas'),
('CHL', '152', 'Chile', -70.6, -33.5, 35, 25, 40, 30, 10, array['Cu','Li','Au','Ag']::text[], 'Largest copper producer in the world and second in lithium. Institutions are strong; the live risks are permitting time, water, grade decline at Codelco and the state''s role in lithium through the Codelco–SQM joint venture. The government that took office in March 2026 has promised faster permitting (verify programme).', array['Permitting reform implementation and project timelines','Codelco output recovery after the El Teniente accident','Codelco–SQM lithium JV and new salar tenders','Water and desalination constraints in the north']::text[], '[["2027", "Codelco structural projects: Chuquicamata underground, El Teniente new mine level"], ["2029-11", "Presidential election"]]'::jsonb, array['metals']::text[], 'americas'),
('PER', '604', 'Peru', -75, -10, 65, 50, 45, 50, 20, array['Cu','Au','Zn','Ag','Fe','Crude','Gas','Prod']::text[], 'Second-largest copper producer with a chronically unstable presidency: seven presidents since 2016. The mining belt works despite the politics, but the southern road corridor from Las Bambas is blocked several times a year and informal gold mining (REINFO) is now a security problem. The 2026 general election produced a new government in July (desk to confirm).', array['Las Bambas corridor blockades and the REINFO formalisation deadline','New administration''s mining and security posture (inaugurated 28 July 2026, verify)','Chancay port ramp-up and Cosco exclusivity','Norperuano pipeline attacks and Petroperú finances']::text[], '[["2026-12", "REINFO extension decision (verify)"], ["2027", "T\u00eda Mar\u00eda construction milestones"]]'::jsonb, array['metals','energy']::text[], 'americas'),
('BOL', '068', 'Bolivia', -64.5, -17, 60, 40, 70, 60, 20, array['Li','Zn','Ag','3T','Au','Gas']::text[], 'Holder of the largest lithium resource and producer of almost none of it. Twenty years of MAS rule ended in November 2025; the new government inherits a dollar shortage, fuel queues and declining gas output. Lithium contracts with Chinese and Russian partners await legislative approval.', array['Ratification of YLB lithium contracts (CBC, Uranium One)','Fuel import financing and diesel shortages','Gas export volumes to Brazil and Argentina','Exchange-rate unification']::text[], '[["2026-12", "Hydrocarbons law revision expected (verify)"], ["2027", "Uyuni direct-lithium-extraction pilot results"]]'::jsonb, array['metals','energy']::text[], 'americas'),
('COL', '170', 'Colombia', -73, 4, 55, 60, 55, 50, 25, array['Au','Ni','Crude','Gas','Prod']::text[], 'Declining oil and gas producer with the region''s most active insurgent threat to pipelines. The Petro government froze new exploration licences and Colombia became a gas importer; the 2026 election (31 May, runoff 21 June) decides whether that policy reverses (desk to confirm result). US relations deteriorated sharply in late 2025.', array['New government''s stance on exploration licensing (inaugurated 7 August 2026, verify)','ELN attacks on Caño Limón–Coveñas and Catatumbo security','Gas import capacity at Cartagena and Buenaventura','US sanctions exposure after the October 2025 designations']::text[], '[["2026-11", "Peace-talks framework review with ELN (verify)"], ["2027", "Reficar and Barrancabermeja refinery turnarounds"]]'::jsonb, array['metals','energy']::text[], 'americas'),
('VEN', '862', 'Venezuela', -66, 7.5, 90, 75, 85, 65, 95, array['Crude','Prod','Gas','Au','Fe','Bx']::text[], 'Largest oil reserves in the world and output under one million b/d. The January 2026 US operation that removed Maduro left an interim administration negotiating sanctions relief licence by licence (verify current status). Every cargo depends on an OFAC authorisation, and the grid, refineries and upgraders are degraded.', array['OFAC general licences and who may lift crude','Interim government stability and election timetable','Orinoco upgrader throughput and Jose terminal loadings','Gold exports from the Orinoco Mining Arc']::text[], '[["2026-12", "OFAC licence renewals (verify dates)"], ["2027", "Transition election timetable (not fixed)"]]'::jsonb, array['metals','energy']::text[], 'americas'),
('GUY', '328', 'Guyana', -58.9, 5, 30, 30, 30, 45, 10, array['Crude','Au','Bx']::text[], 'Fastest-growing oil producer in the world: Stabroek passed 900,000 b/d in 2025 with Yellowtail and heads towards 1.3 million b/d by 2027. Politics are stable after the September 2025 election; the external risk is Venezuela''s Essequibo claim.', array['Uaru (2026) and Whiptail (2027) start-ups','ICJ ruling on the Essequibo case and Venezuelan posture','Gas-to-energy project and local content rules','Gold mining permits and Omai restart']::text[], '[["2026", "Uaru project first oil"], ["2027", "Whiptail first oil; Stabroek towards 1.3 million b/d"]]'::jsonb, array['metals','energy']::text[], 'americas'),
('ECU', '218', 'Ecuador', -78.2, -1.5, 50, 65, 50, 55, 20, array['Crude','Prod','Au','Cu']::text[], 'Oil exporter whose two trunk pipelines run through landslide country, and whose coast is now a cartel battleground. The Noboa government is pro-investment but faced a month-long national strike in 2025 over fuel subsidies. Mirador and Fruta del Norte anchor a growing mining sector.', array['SOTE and OCP pipeline integrity and force majeure declarations','Cartel violence around Guayaquil and Esmeraldas','Mining consultation rules and Cascabel permitting','Fuel subsidy policy and protest risk']::text[], '[["2027", "Cascabel copper-gold construction decision (verify)"], ["2029-02", "General election"]]'::jsonb, array['metals','energy']::text[], 'americas'),
('PAN', '591', 'Panama', -80.3, 8.6, 40, 25, 50, 35, 15, array['Cu']::text[], 'The canal and a closed copper mine. Cobre Panamá has been shut since the Supreme Court voided its contract in November 2023; the government has allowed preservation work and is negotiating a path to reopen. Canal transits recovered from the 2023–24 drought, but water remains the constraint and the ports concession is caught between Washington and Beijing.', array['Cobre Panamá reopening terms and First Quantum arbitration','Gatún Lake levels through the dry season (January–April)','Panama Ports / CK Hutchison concession outcome','Río Indio reservoir project']::text[], '[["2027-01", "Dry season begins; draft restrictions possible if Gat\u00fan is low"], ["2029-05", "General election"]]'::jsonb, array['metals','energy']::text[], 'americas'),
('TTO', '780', 'Trinidad and Tobago', -61.3, 10.5, 35, 40, 35, 30, 30, array['LNG','Gas','Prod','Crude']::text[], 'The Caribbean''s LNG and petrochemical hub, short of gas. Atlantic LNG runs below capacity; the Dragon cross-border field with Venezuela was blocked when OFAC revoked its licence in April 2025, and its future depends on Caracas–Washington relations.', array['Dragon and Manakin-Cocuina cross-border gas licences','Atlantic LNG train utilisation','Deepwater exploration results (bp, Shell)','Alignment with US policy on Venezuela']::text[], '[["2027", "Manatee field first gas (Shell)"], ["2030-04", "General election"]]'::jsonb, array['energy']::text[], 'americas'),
